Files added to linux-user/ by the SENSES project
------------------------------------------------
The following files do not exist in upstream QEMU. Each carries its own
SPDX tag (GPL-2.0-or-later) and its own copyright line at the top:

    linux-user/guest_alarm.c        linux-user/guest_net.c
    linux-user/guest_ashmem.c       linux-user/guest_oom.c
    linux-user/guest_binder.c       linux-user/guest_pagelog.c
    linux-user/guest_fb.c           linux-user/guest_persona.c
    linux-user/guest_fs.c           linux-user/guest_proc.c
    linux-user/guest_fwinit.c       linux-user/guest_rlimit.c
    linux-user/guest_ids.c          linux-user/guest_script.c
    linux-user/guest_input.c        linux-user/guest_selinux.c
    linux-user/guest_kill.c         linux-user/guest_tls.c
    linux-user/guest_trace.c

and the matching headers: guest_*.h.

Modified upstream files
-----------------------
Upstream files were edited in place to call into the additions above. The
calls sit where a syscall or a translation-block event is intercepted; every
such edit is marked in the source with a comment beginning with a star
character, so a diff against qemu-8.2.4 upstream shows exactly what changed.

Principal places:

    linux-user/syscall.c            syscall interception and dispatch
    linux-user/mmap.c               guest address space, ashmem ranges
    linux-user/elfload.c            guest ELF loading and bionic peculiarities
    linux-user/main.c               option and environment handling
    linux-user/linuxload.c          initial stack and argv/envp layout
    linux-user/arm/cpu_loop.c       ARM-specific traps: cacheflush, set_tls
    accel/tcg/user-exec.c           page protection for self-modifying code

Note on the NOTICE file
-----------------------
The header of every added file says "Everything changed in QEMU is listed in
NOTICE", but no such file was present in the source tree this snapshot was
taken from. This file is that missing notice, written from the tree itself
rather than from the project's own records, so treat it as a description of
what is here, not as the project's authoritative changelog.

